## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply SWAR-style bit counting across a 128-bit structure without branches, using two independent 64-bit halves. Also checks portability when no native 128-bit constant exists.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR popcount on 64-bit chunks
- 128-bit represented as { hi, lo }
- Edge-case handling for 0 and all-ones

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = lo + (lo >> 8);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 16);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 32);

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = hi + (hi >> 8);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 16);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 32);

  uint8_t cl = lo & 0x7F;
  uint8_t ch = hi & 0x7F;
  return cl + ch;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for wider word sizes (e.g., 256-bit)?
- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of compact bit-twiddling techniques to count bits without loops, a common interview staple for performance-critical code.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit counting (SIMD-style within a register)
- Bit masks and shifts for aggregation
- Portability across unsigned integers and compilers

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t n) {
  n = n - ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (n * 0x01010101) >> 24;
}
```

