## Why This Is Asked

The parity check is a common error-detection primitive in data blocks and crypto pipelines. This task tests ability to perform a full 128-bit reduction with pure bitwise ops, no branches, and constant time.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value represented as hi/lo.
- Parity reduction via XOR folding.
- Branchless implementation for performance.

## Code Example

```javascript
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (int)(x & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt parity128 for native 128-bit ints on platforms with __int128?
- What are the trade-offs if you can't rely on unsigned shifts across all compilers?

## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform multi-word arithmetic with pure bitwise/arith operations, handling carry without branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic via two 64-bit halves
- Carry propagation using unsigned overflow properties
- Branchless computation

## Code Example
```c
void add128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  uint64_t lo = a_lo + b_lo;
  uint64_t carry = (lo < a_lo);
  uint64_t hi = a_hi + b_hi + carry;
  *out_hi = hi;
  *out_lo = lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256-bit operands?
- What are the potential pitfalls on platforms with different endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a hardware-style popcount without loops or built-in functions, demonstrating understanding of bitwise manipulation techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting using bitmasks
- 32-bit unsigned arithmetic with logical shifts
- Elimination of branches and loops for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countBits32(n) {
  n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  n = n + (n >>> 8);
  n = n + (n >>> 16);
  return n & 0x3F;
}
```

