## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a branchless, efficient 128-bit bit-count using a proven 64-bit SWAR approach on two halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Treat 128-bit as two independent 64-bit parts
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// C-like helper (conceptual)
static inline uint8_t pop64(uint64_t v){
  v -= (v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
uint8_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world bitfield extraction often needs to pull a contiguous block from a 128-bit value into the least-significant bits for parsing, with no branching for performance.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using two 64-bit halves
- Bit masking and shifting across a 128-bit boundary
- Pure bitwise manipulations (no loops)

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractBitBlock128(uint128 x, unsigned start, unsigned width) {
  // Build mask in 128-bit form: width ones at LSBs
  uint128 M = {0,0};
  if (width == 128) { M.hi = ~0ULL; M.lo = ~0ULL; }
  else if (width < 64) { M.hi = 0; M.lo = (width==0)?0:((1ULL<<width)-1); }
  else { M.lo = ~0ULL; M.hi = (width-64==0)?0:((1ULL<<(width-64))-1); }
  // Shift right by start and apply mask
  // Implement 128-bit shift and AND here respecting the architecture
  uint128 y = {0,0};
  // (Assume helper shifts across hi/lo are available)
  y = shrl128(x, start);
  return and128(y, M);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for width exactly 64 or 128 in terms of branchless masks?
- How would you adapt to architectures lacking native 128-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of efficient bit-hacks and how to compute parity without loops or branching, which is common in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- Bit folding using XOR and shifts to aggregate bit information
- Constant-time parity methods and small lookup tables
- Branchless implementation for predictable performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t parity64(uint64_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return (0x6996 >> (x & 0xF)) & 1;
}
```

